amux device — phones and browsers that reach this machine directly (host only)

usage: amux device <command>

The device listener (the direct server) is an opt-in HTTPS listener on a
private address. A phone or browser connects with a one-time code you paste or
scan (connect --qr), then reaches this host's sessions with destination-issued
credentials. No cloud service is in
the path. See docs/direct-access.md.

Disabling the managed listener is not revocation: clients stay enrolled and
the cloud policy stays as it is. Use amux device revoke and amux cloud
enroll disable for those.

commands:
  connect [flags]       connect a phone or browser: a one-time code to paste, or a QR code to scan
  ls [--stale [--idle-days N]] [--json]
                        list connected devices (enrolled clients), with any in-place grant changes
  update <client-id> [--permissions <list>] [--all-sessions | --workgroup <id>...]
                        change a device's permissions or scope in place (no reconnect needed)
  revoke <client-id>    disconnect a device now (closes its streams first)
  status [--json]       setting, managed listener, server and provider offer
  enable [flags]        save the listener settings and turn it on (the daemon runs it from now on)
  disable [--no-wait]   turn the managed listener off (settings, clients and cloud policy are kept)
  logs [-n lines]       the managed listener's log
  audit [--client <id>] [--limit N] [--json]
                        answers, item triage and overseer steers made by direct clients (newest last)
  serve [flags]         run the device listener in the foreground (manual)
  reset-identity --yes  replace the server identity; revokes every client and disables cloud enrollment

Run 'amux device <command> --help' for details.
